<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgundians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Burgundians were an East Germanic people who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the early 5th century and founded a kingdom as Roman allies around 411, traditionally centred on Worms. After its destruction by Hunnic troops in Roman service in 436, the survivors were settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapaudia">Sapaudia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 443, where they built a new kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> valleys. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> conquered it in 534, and its name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgundians.jpg" alt="Burgundians" width="320" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Altaileopard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conquests_of_Clovis.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Burgundians are one of the disappearing and reappearing tribes listed by the historian Peter Heather, which appear in Roman records in one place and reappear much later in another. The earliest named by Greco-Roman writers lived near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> between the first and third centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> lists the Burgodiones among the Vandili. Roman panegyrics report that in the late third century they suffered devastating defeats by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, which may have driven some of them west. They later appear near the Main, and in the fourth century they became Roman allies against the Alemanni. Early in the fifth century, in a period of wider crisis, the Gibichung-led Burgundians crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and settled within the empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> kingdom, probably near Worms, was destroyed in 436 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and their Hun allies killed many Burgundians together with their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundahar">Gundahar</a>. His death became a central theme in medieval Germanic heroic legend, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>, where he is Günther. The Gallic Chronicle of 452 records that in 443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapaudia">Sapaudia</a> was given to the remainder of the Burgundians, to be divided with the native inhabitants; the territory included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> became the capital. Ian N. Wood believes that a true Burgundian kingdom emerged only between 474 and 494.</p><p>After about 480 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundobad">Gundobad</a> became principal king, sharing power with his brothers. Civil war broke out in 500 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godegisel">Godegisel</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and defeated Gundobad; with Visigothic support Gundobad regained control in 501. Gundobad died in 516 and left the throne to his Catholic son Sigismund. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish empire</a> incorporated the kingdom in 534.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Burgundians left several legal codes, which are among the fullest evidence for how the early successor states legislated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gundobad">Gundobad</a> published edicts concerning relations between Burgundians and Roman citizens, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, the Lex Romana Burgundionum. Little is known of their language beyond some names and a few words in legal texts, but it was Germanic. The court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> had an Arian faction, which held sway over some kings, including Gundobad, but several women of the royal family were Catholics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> is said to have been converted by his Burgundian wife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burgundians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Clovis’s sons destroyed the Kingdom of the Thuringians in 531 and the Kingdom of the <b>Burgundians</b> in 534 and obtained Provence from the Ostrogoths in 537, and Frankish armies intervened repeatedly in Italy in the sixth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks under Clovis I ended this expansion: in 507, allied with the <b>Burgundians</b>, they killed Alaric II at the Battle of Campus Vogladensis near Poitiers and sacked Toulouse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Burgundians</b> under Maximilian of Habsburg, the future Emperor Maximilian I, defeated the French troops of King Louis XI.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Burgundians</b> took Paris during the civil war, a mob killed Bernard VII, Count of Armagnac and constable of France, together with his sympathisers, all prisoners, foreign bankers, and students and faculty of the College of Navarre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bernard VII became constable of France and led the Armagnac party against the <b>Burgundians</b> in the civil war of the 1410s until his murder in Paris in 1418.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-armagnac">County of Armagnac</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Burgundians</b> crossed the Rhine in the early 5th century, and in 411 their king Gundahar set up a realm on the Rhine, traditionally placed around Worms, as Roman allies.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-burgundians">Kingdom of the Burgundians</a></li></ul></section>
